The Avalonia desktop shell and launch-page layout in
`src/PCL.Aurora.Desktop/Views/MainWindow.axaml` adapts the PCL2 title-bar,
navigation-control and launch-page XAML references listed above. The title-bar
gradient follows PCL2 `ModSecret.vb`; it does not include the upstream WPF
implementation. Aurora retains its own bindings and cross-platform window
behavior.
The software-information legal section and `PclCollapsibleCard` adapt the
three-card organization, 40-pixel collapsed title area, title-only hit testing,
arrow rotation timing and distance-sensitive height motion from PCL-CE
`PageSetupAbout.xaml` and `MyCard.cs`. The legal wording and dependency list
describe PCL Aurora's actual data flow, upstream use and distributed packages;
they do not copy PCL-CE service claims that Aurora does not implement.
The community-resource version organizer and dependency resolver adapt the
filter, card-grouping, dependency classification and pre-download confirmation
rules from the PCL-CE `PageDownloadCompDetail` and `ModComp` sources listed
above. Network requests, immutable models, file dialogs and verified downloads
are implemented through Aurora's cross-platform application boundaries.
The world catalog adapts PCL-CE `PageDownloadWorld` categories and the
CurseForge search, project and file mappings in `ModComp`. Aurora routes those
credential-free requests through the public mirror used by PCL-CE, parses them
into Aurora's own immutable models, and imports verified world ZIP files through
a transactional cross-platform extractor. No PCL or PCL-CE API credential is
copied, embedded or distributed by PCL Aurora.
The manual Java import flow adapts PCL2 `ModJava.ManuallyImportJava` and
PCL-CE `PageSetupJava.BtnAdd_Click`: the user selects an executable, Aurora
executes the existing platform inspector, validates version and architecture,
deduplicates the result and stores only the local executable path. The homepage
loader adapts PCL-CE `PageLaunchRight` and `PageSetupUI`, while rendering remote
content through Aurora's restricted XML control allowlist rather than executing
arbitrary XAML. Preset addresses and the Minecraft news allowlist follow the
listed PCL-CE sources.
The runtime launch-patch flow adapts PCL-CE `ModLaunch`: LegacyFix and the
LWJGL unsafe agent are selected from effective version metadata, extracted
atomically into the platform cache and injected through argument lists without
shell joining. Windows Java executable and GPU preference behavior follows the
listed PCL/PCL-CE launch helpers, while Linux uses PRIME environment variables
